      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f21461c1-5260-5bab-979a-65a5c252d219",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}-(2)xy?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(2,1)$,$(-2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=\\frac{3}{2}-2(3)(1)=\\frac{3}{2}-(6)=- \\frac{9}{2}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=\\frac{3}{2}-2(2)(1)=\\frac{3}{2}-(4)=- \\frac{5}{2}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=\\frac{3}{2}-2(-2)(1)=\\frac{3}{2}-(-4)=\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-27-topic-audit-2/published-probe/17592186172038/79c6129d09f7b3b96e5913911af860a0f80dbfb6209920c86bbd937e87feaa86.png)",
